  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5b5fce92",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Relationship behavior patterns (Gottman-style indicators):** every negative behavior is higher, on average, in divorced couples — criticism, contempt, defensiveness, and stonewalling all increase, while the positive indicators (repair attempt success, positive-to-negative ratio, shared activities) all decrease. Contempt shows the largest relative jump (2.59 → 4.04), which lines up with Gottman's own research identifying contempt as the most corrosive of the \"Four Horsemen.\" The boxplots confirm this isn't just a mean-shift artifact — divorced couples show a visibly higher and wider spread of contempt scores, and a lower, tighter spread of repair attempt success."
   ]
  },
