   "execution_count": 22,
   "id": "73b8f6db",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "years_married  years_to_divorce    1.0\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 22,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "feature_corr = df[numeric_cols].corr().abs()\n",
    "\n",
    "upper_triangle = feature_corr.where(\n",
    "    np.triu(np.ones(feature_corr.shape), k=1).astype(bool)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "high_corr_pairs = (\n",
    "    upper_triangle.stack()\n",
    "    .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "high_corr_pairs[high_corr_pairs > 0.8]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5f1d289d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Correlation with the target:** contempt has the strongest positive correlation with divorce (0.33), followed by criticism, stonewalling, and defensiveness — consistent with the group-mean differences above. `years_married` shows the strongest correlation overall (-0.35), but negatively: longer marriages in this sample are less likely to be divorced, which is exactly the kind of relationship we need to be careful with (see below). Weak or near-zero correlations (age gap, cohabitation, employment, number of children) suggest those fields won't carry much predictive weight individually, though they could still interact with other features.\n",
    "\n",
    "**High correlation pairs:** the only pair above 0.8 is `years_married` and `years_to_divorce`, at a perfect 1.0. That's expected — `years_to_divorce` is presumably only populated for divorced couples and is mechanically tied to `years_married`. This confirms the leakage concern flagged earlier and justifies dropping both from the feature set below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2c1aeb26",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Feature Selection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 23,
   "id": "14e2e5ea",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "target = \"divorced\"\n",
    "drop_cols = [\n",
    "    \"marriage_id\",\n",
    "    \"years_to_divorce\",\n",
    "    \"years_married\",\n",
    "    target\n",
    "]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f8cf6273",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Feature selection rationale:**\n",
    "- `marriage_id` — a unique identifier, carries no predictive signal.\n",
    "- `years_to_divorce` — only defined for marriages that ended in divorce, so it's unavailable at prediction time for an ongoing marriage and would leak the target directly.\n",
    "- `years_married` — perfectly correlated with `years_to_divorce` (1.0) and strongly correlated with the target itself (-0.35). Whether a marriage has *already* lasted N years is entangled with whether it ends in divorce by construction, so it's excluded as a leakage risk rather than a genuine predictive signal known at marriage time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 24,
   "id": "ddb2ce59",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "X=df.drop(columns=drop_cols)\n",
    "y=df[target]"
   ]
  },
