   "source": [
    "## Project Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. What does one row represent?** A single marriage record, with demographic, financial, relationship-behavior, and outcome fields.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. What are we trying to predict?** Whether the marriage ended in divorce (`divorced`: 0/1).\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Is the target balanced?** Reasonably — about 54% not divorced vs. 46% divorced, close enough that no resampling was needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. Which columns should not be used as model features?** `marriage_id` (no signal), and `years_to_divorce` / `years_married` (both leak information tied to the outcome — `years_to_divorce` is only defined post-divorce, and `years_married` is perfectly correlated with it).\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. Why is `years_to_divorce` missing for many rows?** Because it's only recorded for marriages that actually ended in divorce — the missingness itself is directly tied to the target (\"missing not at random\"), which is precisely why it can't be used as a feature.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key findings:**\n",
    "- Relationship behavior indicators (contempt, criticism, defensiveness, stonewalling, repair attempts) are the strongest predictors — contempt alone has the highest single correlation with divorce (0.33).\n",
    "- Education level shows a strong, monotonic protective effect: divorce rate falls from ~63% (less than high school) to ~30% (graduate degree).\n",
    "- Religious attendance, despite being an intuitive candidate feature, shows almost no separation between classes (45–47% divorce rate across all categories).\n",
    "- Logistic Regression (76% accuracy, 0.84 ROC AUC) performed marginally better than Random Forest, suggesting the underlying relationships are close to linear/additive.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:**\n",
    "- Results come from a single 80/20 train/test split; a k-fold cross-validation would give a more robust comparison between models.\n",
    "- This is an observational dataset — none of the correlations here imply causation (e.g. \"more contempt causes divorce\" is not something this analysis can establish).\n",
    "- As noted at the top of the notebook, this is a learning exercise in applied data science, not a basis for real-life relationship advice."
   ]
  }
